+#### Pruning finished branches and worktrees
+
+Merged branches and the worktrees made for them pile up unless something removes them. The `branches` group does it for you:
+
+```bash
+ta cleanup --only branches --dry-run   # Every branch, why it would be deleted or kept, and its tip
+ta cleanup --only branches             # Delete the safe ones, ask about the rest
 ```
 
+The pull request state from your VCS (GitHub, through `gh`) decides, not `git branch -d`, which calls a squash-merged branch unmerged. A local branch is deleted without asking when:
+
+- its PR is merged and the branch has no commits beyond the PR head, or
+- every commit on it is already in the default branch (a brand new branch with no commits of its own is kept).
+
+If the branch is checked out in a linked worktree, the worktree is removed too, but only when it has no uncommitted or untracked files. Worktree records whose directory is already gone are pruned as well.
+
+These are never deleted automatically. They are listed as questions, with the reason, and the default answer is to keep them:
+
+- The branch you are on, the default branch, and a branch checked out in the main worktree (never touched at all).
+- A branch whose PR is still open, closed without merging, or that has no PR and unmerged commits.
+- A merged branch with commits beyond the PR head (the commits are listed).
+- A branch whose worktree has uncommitted or untracked files (the files are listed) or is locked.
+- A branch whose PR state could not be looked up (for example `gh` is not signed in).
+
+Every deletion is recorded in `.ta/audit.jsonl` with the branch name and its full tip SHA. To restore one: `git branch <name> <sha>`.
+
+Remote branches are never deleted by cleanup (let GitHub's "Automatically delete head branches" do that). Remote branches whose PR was closed without merging are listed as notes so you can review them.
+
+The group runs by itself in three places, with no flag needed:
+
+- Right after `ta draft watch` or `ta draft merge` sees your PR merge.
+- On every `ta cleanup`.
+- In the daemon's periodic cleanup, even when `auto = "off"`. With `auto = "safe"` it runs alongside the other groups.
+
+Set `branches = false` under `[cleanup]` to turn all of that off. `ta cleanup --only branches` still runs it on request.
+
 ### Unified Garbage Collection (`ta gc`)
 
 Run all cleanup tasks in a single pass — zombie goals, stale staging directories, and orphaned draft packages:
